A||||4||Philphine||tranny redline?||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||09-27-2001||07:44 AM||||<BR>is there such a thing or is it something to be considered in the event i found a fairly high rpm engine? any guess at what it might be if so? specifically for a 4:10. thanks for any help, thoughts and/or opinions.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||09-27-2001||09:28 PM||||say?<P>i occured to me to wonder when we were working with a gear box at work. they have rpm limits specified for the motors they are used with. would a car tranny have anything like that?||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||09-28-2001||11:26 AM||||My <B>guess</B> is that in <B>most</B> situations, the rev limits of the engine configuration, and of the flywheel construction, are ordinarily reached long before a transmission's rpm limit...unless you're thinking of using really wild stuff, like a gas turbine engine and filament-wound graphite flywheel  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I don't have enough data (translation: what I said, plus I'm too lazy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) to calculate the critical speeds for the transaxle, which would define one of the rpm limitations.  Another limitation would involve wear, but to calculate that, we'd need to know what sort of torque we're transmitting, how long you want it to live, etc.<P>Kind of a generic answer, I know, but you said you were after thoughts and/or opinions.  And my <B>opinion</B> (based on like, no data) is that, for the most part, you needn't worry about rpm limits for the transaxle itself.<P>{Cue for people to pipe in with rpm-related transmission failures  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)    <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||09-28-2001||05:34 PM||||ok. i just wanted a more educated guess than i could take at it. along with the gear boxes at work, i was also thinking about that my 4.10 was originaly mated to an engine with a 4.5k redline. since there are engines that can come close to doubling that, it made me wonder if it needed to be considered.||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||09-29-2001||12:00 AM||||Generally, you won't have a problem unless you're going to spend much time above 8,000rpm (after that, we're basically talking a grey area involving objective determinations from a bunch of intersecting subjective curves.  How much wear is acceptable?  How long a life is ok past 8,000? and a thousand other factors like existing wear, tranny fluid used, etc.)<P>Basically, if you're not putting in a race engine, you have no need to worry about it.<P>(yeah, the duke really sucks major ass, I know I had one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
